WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.500
*music plays*

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.840
[British voice-over] In a few moments, he will have written the funniest joke in the world...

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:07.860
...and, as a consequence, he will die...laughing.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.060
*laughs*

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:16.860
*laughing intensifies*

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:36.600
First off, I think it's wrong to say either style of comedy is better.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.740
I find it kind of annoying when people say British comedy is always smarter, or wittier.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:44.760
Have you seen 'Arrested Development' or '21 Jump Street' - or even a 'Spongebob'-episode?

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.700
"Be careful, Patrick. Being an artist is a heavy responsibility."

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.100
"Each work of art is like a child, and must be treated as such."

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.920
"Come on, I was just going to draw a cartoon!"

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.780
"Okay! Why didn't you say so?!"

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.800
[Narrator] There's definitely very smart writing on both sides of the Atlantic,

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.480
but that doesn't answer the question:

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:02.580
What makes them different?

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:06.260
After weeks of research, my answer is still "I don't know," but let's give it a shot.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.760
[British voice-over] "No one could read it and live."

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:11.940
*gasps*

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.320
*begins laughing*

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:31.980
*laughing intensifies*

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:32.560
*dies*

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.540
[Ricky Gervais] No, I don't change it, I mean..

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.520
..the difference, if we're talking about the big two - Britain and America -

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:41.940
Sorry, rest of the world.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:46.000
[Narrator] I think the first step is to take a British comedy and see how it's American adaptation changed.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:49.140
"What's the difference between David Brent and Michael Scott?"

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:53.260
By season 2 the U.S. "Office" begins it's movement away from the U.K. original.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:56.880
We even see a subtle cinematic change by brightening the lights in the office,

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:59.940
while originally just a way to differentiate it from the UK version...

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:03.580
...the added brightness seems to serve as a nice metaphor for the adaptation as a whole.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:08.980
[Ricky Gervais] But the big difference is that Americans are more optimistic.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:18.920
and that's due to the fact that Americans are told they can become the next president of the United States, and....they can.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:24.260
British people are told "it won't happen to you", and they carry that, they carry that with them.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:29.040
There's no doubt that America lightened up the show. Michael Scott had his failures but there's a hope to it.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:34.000
In one episode he wasn't invited to Jim's party, but shows up anyway, genuinely hurt and bitter.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.000
"Wow! Who opened up the morgue for this thing!"

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.200
[Narrator] He picks a duet song and no one will sing it with him.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:44.380
Reluctantly Jim joins in, giving Michael a big, cheesy smile and an overall happy ending to the episode.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:48.000
But when David Brent preforms it's still funny, but a different kind of funny.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:51.940
Nobody pities him or cheers him on. They all just watch in embarrassment.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:03:03.980
*dancing and grunting noises*

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:05.860
[Narrator] There's no happy ending and it's not very hopeful.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:07.860
Or look at how both characters wrap up their time on the show.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.140
- spoiler alert -

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:12.180
Michael Scott quits to move with his fiancee to Colorado...

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:13.940
...and David Brent begs for his job back.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:15.940
[Brent] I will try twice as hard.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:17.720
I really will. I know I've been complacent..

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:19.400
..and I will turn this place around

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:22.140
If we just say that it is not definite now.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:25.520
[Narrator] Of course this doesn't include the Christmas special, which was a lot happier.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:29.340
[Fry] Put it this way: the American comic here like John Belushi or someone like that is to

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:34.640
You know that scene in 'Animal House' where the player -

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:37.940
a fellow playing folk music on a guitar.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:42.500
And John Belushi picks up the guitar and destroys it.

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.140
And the cinema loved it. He just smashes it and then waggles his...

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.980
...eye brows up the camera and went: 'God, it's so great.'

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:52.220
Well, the British comedian would want to play the folk singer.

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:54.540
*Laughs*

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:56.420
We want to play the failure.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:04.280
*Singing*

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:08.660
[Narrator] So maybe the difference is the optimistic American dream versus the dark British reality.

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:10.660
At least that's what many people think about it.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:14.060
[Fry] Europe is just basically a gene pool of people whose ancestors said:

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.840
Oh, I don't think I can risk - there, I can't risk it.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:20.300
America is a gene pool -  people say: let's risk it!

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:21.840
Let's try it!

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.680
[Narrator] But I think that answer is too easy, because you have to ignore the fact that

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:27.880
Americans love the failures, like Shaun from Shaun of the Dead-

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:32.740
-and plenty of modern American sit-coms feature losers who never get their redemption.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:37.120
*BGM*

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:39.120
Frankly, I think it's an outdated theory.

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:43.400
The past television era of "Married With Children" and "Seinfeld" and "Friends"

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:48.180
always featured the classic wise guy who always had the funny one-liner for every moment.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:50.100
[Seinfeld] So you know how to take the reservation-

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.940
you just don't know how to "hold" the reservation?

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:53.620
*Laughs*

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:55.040
[Narrator] But I think in the last ten years

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:59.180
we're starting to see a self-awareness of this "American Dream optimism"-style of comedy.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:02.580
In "Its Always Sunny [In Philadelphia]" there certainly is that feeling that feeling of hope in the characters.

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:04.400
They think they can accomplish anything.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:09.240
But they always take it way too far by becoming violent, cruel and hilariously backstabbing.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:13.880
It's almost a critique on 'American Optimism'. In America you're suppose to succeed, but at what cost?

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:16.720
[male actor] Look, if we use a gun we're definitely gonna need a silencer.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:17.840
[other actor] A gun?

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:20.240
[male actor] Fine, no guns,no guns. Err.. piano wire, OK, maybe we can use the piano wire.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:24.400
Look, whatever it is, I gotta be the guy to pull the trigger on this thing.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:27.920
[Narrator] Not to mention they always fail and face the consequences.

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:32.480
And it seems like not one single thing ever works out for Larry in "Curb Your Enthusiasm".

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:35.840
[Larry] I need them!! [Officer] Step out of the car, please. [Larry] Oh, come on! Are you serious?!

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:41.360
[Narrator] And an episode of "Louie" ends with his date running into a helicopter to escape him. It doesn't get more pathetic than that.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:46.320
So maybe some American comedy pokes fun at the hope and light-hearted nature of past sit-coms.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:50.000
Or maybe American comedy is even shifting towards that British cynicism.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.280
Or maybe we'll always hold that American optimism in our hearts.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.520
And even in the millennial generation, where the American Dream seems to do nothing but screw us over,

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:01.680
but it'll always be ingrained in our culture and mindset to "shrug it off" and keep on trying.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:05.360
[male actor] I'm not going to take "no" for an answer because I just refuse to do that because I'm a winner,

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:09.760
and winners- we don't listen to words like "no" or "don't" or "stop!"

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:12.440
Those words are just not in our vocabulary.

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:16.200
[Narrator] I know this a topic a lot of people get passionate about, but I hope the comment section...

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:19.260
doesn't get too heated. At least, I don't expect some kind of Spanish Inquisition.

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:21.200
[in scene] *dramatic music plays*

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:24.720
[lead actor] Nobody expects the Spanish Inquisition!

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.380
[Narrator] Thanks for watching!

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:27.840
*music*

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:33.840
www.patreon.com/nowyouseeit

